CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to seek to strengthen the competitiveness of the defense industry, and to ultimately contribute to the promotion of the advanced strong military and development of the national economy, by prescribing matters concerning the execution of defense acquisition programs, such as the improvement of defense capabilities, promotion of the defense industry, procurement of military supplies, etc., in order to establish a foundation for self-reliant national defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 31, 2010]

Article 2 (Fundamental concepts) The fundamental concepts of this Act are to expand systems and capabilities for the defense acquisition programs to ensure national security, and to maintain preparedness for self-reliant national defense and expand potential for economic growth by strengthening the competitiveness of the defense industry through securing of transparency, specialty, and efficiency of defense acquisition programs.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 31, 2010]

Article 3 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Apr. 1, 2009; May 9, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Mar. 21, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. "Defense force improvement project" means the purchasing and research and development including new development, improvement of performance, etc., of weapons systems for enhancing military force, and the installation, etc. of facilities incidental thereto;
  2. "Military supplies" means materials acquired to be used and administered by the Ministry of National Defense and military units or organizations under the direct control of the Ministry, or army, navy, and air force (hereinafter referred to as "each service"), which are classified into weapons systems and force support systems;
  3. "Weapons systems" means the integration of weapons that show fighting capability in the battlefield, such as guided weapons, aircraft, naval vessels, etc., and all sorts of elements, such as equipment, parts, facilities, software, etc. necessary for the operation thereof, which are prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  4. "Force support system" means all sorts of elements, such as equipment, parts, facilities, software, other materials, etc. other than a weapons system;
  5. "Acquisition" means procurement of military supplies by purchasing (including lease; hereinafter the same shall apply) or procurement through research, development, and manufacture;
  6. "Offset trade" means trade where a condition of reciprocal service, such as transfer of relevant knowledge, technology, etc., the overseas export of domestic weapons, equipment, parts, etc., is attached when purchasing weapons, equipment, etc. from abroad;
  7. "Defense materials" means materials designated pursuant to Article 34 among military supplies;
  8. "Defense industry" means the defense industry defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  9. "Defense contractor" means an enterprise that manufactures defense materials, so designated pursuant to Article 35;
  9-2. "General enterprise" means an enterprise which is not a defense contractor, which is related to the defense industry;
  9-3. "General enterprise which has no relationship with the defense industry" means an enterprise which is not a defense contractor or a general enterprise, which supplies military supplies;
  10. "Specialized research institute" means an organization entrusted by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration with the research, development, testing, and measurement of defense materials, the manufacture and authorization of machinery and tools for testing defense materials, and the development of software relating to the business analysis of defense contractors or the defense industry;
  10-2. "General research institute" means a research institute which is not a specialized research institute;
  11. "Defense industry facilities" means land and fixtures (including equipment and instruments) thereon, which are provided for the research, development, or manufacture of defense materials by defense contractors or specialized research institutes;
  12. "Military supply sales agency business" means the business of intermediating or acting for a foreign enterprise throughout the course of the conclusion of a contract for the conclusion of the contract by and between the foreign enterprise and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration and in the course of performance of the contract.
  13. "Supporting element of force integration" means the following elements that shall be secured for the force integration of weapons systems so that the weapons system is operated promptly upon acquisition and deployment:
  a. The following elements to facilitate combat development to ensure that the acquired weapons systems demonstrate instantaneous fighting capability on the battlefield:
  1) The hardware, software, etc. necessary for the interoperability of the appurtenant facilities and weapons systems;
  2) Military doctrine, and organization and equipment for the formation of military units, education and training, and radio frequencies;
  b. Integrated product support elements, such as parts for repair and operating manuals that are necessary for the systematic management of the acquired weapons system throughout the entire life cycle;
  14. "Procurement contract for national defense" means a contract for the acquisition of military supplies;
  15. "Defense acquisition contract" means a procurement contract for national defense concluded in relation to the following:
  a. A defense research and development project under subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act.
  b. Supporting element of force integration, which is essential for the mass production and operation of weapons systems (excluding appurtenant facilities, military doctrine, organization and equipment for the formulation of military units, education and training, and frequencies), maintenance-related equipment or repair services;
  c. Defense industrial materials (hereinafter referred to as "defense materials");
  d. Military supplies prescribed by Presidential Decree to respond to serious security threats, terrorism, and other emergencies;
  e. Military supplies prescribed by Presidential Decree, which are directly related to the life and safety of military personnel;
  16. "Long-term contract" means a procurement contract for national defense which covers a term of two or more fiscal years, which is prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  17. "Counter-party to a defense acquisition contract" means a person, corporation, or organization that enters into a defense acquisition contract with the State.

Article 4 (Relationship to other statutes) Except as otherwise provided in other statutes regarding defense acquisition programs, the provisions of this Act shall apply.

CHAPTER II TRANSPARENCY AND SPECIALIZATION IN EXECUTION OF DEFENSE AQUISITION PROGRAMS

Article 5 (Real-name policy system and information disclosure) (1) With regard to the determination or execution of major policies on defense acquisition programs, the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall implement the real-name policy system that records and preserves the matters, such as the post, rank, name, and opinion of the participants in the determination or implementation of major policies, various types of plans and reports, the details of discussions and conclusions of meetings, public hearings, etc.
  (2) In promoting defense acquisition programs, the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall disclose information on procedures and details of decision making. In such cases, the Official Information Disclosure Act shall apply to information disclosure.
  (3) Matters necessary for methods, etc. for implementing the real-name policy system pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) The Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall submit, without delay, to the relevant Standing Committee of the National Assembly, the outcomes of analyses and evaluation, and outcomes of incorporating them into polices, in respect of defense force improvement projects, the total project cost of which is at least 500 billion won (50 billion won in cases of research and development), among the outcomes of analyses and evaluation conducted pursuant to Article 23 and 24. <Added on Mar. 31, 2010; May 9, 2014>

Article 6 (Integrity pledge system and ombudsman system) (1) In order to enhance transparency and fairness in the execution of defense acquisition programs, the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall request each of the following persons to submit a pledge of integrity, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, he or she shall require persons specified in subparagraph 6 to submit a pledge of integrity, respectively, when they enter into a subcontract or a sub-subcontract: <Amended on May 9, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Dec. 20, 2016; Mar. 21, 2017; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. Public officials designated by the Minister of National Defense, from among public officials affiliated with the Ministry of National Defense, and public officials affiliated with the Defense Acquisition Program Administration;
  2. The members of any of the following committees:
  a. The Defense Acquisition Program Promotion Committee under Article 9;
  b. Subcommittees and working committees under Article 10;
  c. Committees prescribed by Presidential Decree, which conducts preliminary examinations and advisory services on bids, successful bids, or the conclusion and performance of defense acquisition contracts.
  3. Officers and staff of the Agency for Defense Development provided for in the Act on the Agency for Defense Development (hereinafter referred to as the "Agency for Defense Development") and the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality provided for in Article 32;
  4. The representatives and executive officers of the following enterprises or research institutes participating in the relevant defense acquisition programs:
  a. Defense contractors (hereinafter referred to as "defense contractors");
  b. General enterprises;
  c. General enterprises which have no relationship with the defense industry;
  d. Specialized research institutes;
  e. General research institutes;
  5. The representative and executive officers of each military supply sales agency;
  6. The representative and executive officers of a subcontractor (in cases of a contract for sale, referring to each supplier; hereinafter referred to as "subcontractor") that enters into a subcontract concerning a procurement contract for national defense (including a contract for sale, and limited to the contract amount not less than the amount prescribed by Presidential Decree, which is at least one billion won) with a defense contractor, general enterprise, general enterprise not related to the defense industry, specialized research institute, or general research institute that enters into a procurement contract for national defense; and the representative and executive officers of each sub-subcontractor (in cases of a contract for sale, referring to each supplier) that enters into a sub-subcontract concerning a procurement contract for national defense (including a contract for sale, and limited to the contract whose amount is at least the amount prescribed by Presidential Decree, which is at least one billion won) with the aforesaid subcontractor.
  (2) A pledge of integrity referred to in paragraph (1) shall contain the following: <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. Matters regarding prohibition, etc. of demands for, promises of, giving and receiving of valuables, entertainment, etc.;
  2. Matters regarding the prohibition of any unfair practices that unfairly hinder free competition in bidding, such as revealing bid prices in advance and collusion for a successful bid by a specific person;
  3. Matters regarding the prohibition of intercession or solicitation that hinders impartiality in performing duties;
  4. Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree regarding the prohibition, etc. of provision of information related to defense acquisition programs;
  5. Matters regarding the prohibition of profiteering by the person himself or herself, or by a third person, by taking advantage of his or her position;
  6. Matters regarding the prohibition of unfair subcontracting;
  7. Matters regarding the prohibition of violation of obligation under the Defense Technology Security Act and the Military Secret Protection Act.
  (3) Where a person commissioned as a member of the Defense Acquisition Program Promotion Committee pursuant to subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1) fails to comply with the terms and conditions of the pledge of integrity, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall decommission such person. <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (4) Where a person who falls under paragraph (1)4 or 5 violates the terms and conditions of a pledge of integrity, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall revoke the relevant bid or successful bid, or cancel or terminate the relevant contract; provided, where such measure is deemed to inflict significant harm on the public interest in consideration of the circumstances, such as the severity of misconduct like offering money or other valuables and entertainment, the performance progress of the relevant contract, and the scale of loss to the State caused by suspended performance of the contract, the relevant bid, successful bid, or contract may be continuously performed, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (5) In order to enhance transparency and fairness in the execution of defense acquisition programs, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may operate an ombudsman system in which an ombudsman may investigate civil petitions raised in the course of execution of defense acquisition programs and file a request for rectification or inspection. <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (6) A person eligible to be an ombudsman shall satisfy any of the following qualifications; provided, a person cannot be an ombudsman if the person in question, the person's spouse, or the person's lineal descendant or ascendant held office as an executive officer or employee of a defense contractor, general enterprise, general enterprise not related to the defense industry, specialized research institute, general research institute, or military supply sales agency within two years before the person is commissioned as an ombudsman: <Added on Mar. 31, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. A person who held or is holding a position as an associate professor or higher in a department related to the defense industry, or department of accounting, law, or public administration, at a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
  2. An attorney-at-law, certified public accountant, professional engineer, or patent attorney with experience of at least three years in the relevant field;
  3. A person of high integrity who held office as a public official of at least Grade IV (including a member of the Senior Executive Service) at a central administrative agency;
  4. Others persons of learning and high moral repute with abundant expert knowledge and experience in the field of the defense industry.
  (7) An ombudsman may investigate civil petitions and request for rectification, inspection, etc. to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration pursuant to paragraph (5); provided, no ombudsman shall investigate any of the following: <Added on Mar. 31, 2010; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. Matters for which objection or remedy proceedings, such as an administrative appeal, administrative litigation, judgment of the Constitutional Court, are pending under other statutes;
  2. Matters which have become final and conclusive through judgment, decision, adjudication, reconciliation, mediation, arbitration, etc.;
  3. Matters which were or are being inspected by a national agency, such as the Board of Audit and Inspection;
  4. Matters under investigation by an investigative agency.
  (8) When an ombudsman intends to conduct an investigation under paragraph (7), he or she may hear the statement of a relevant employee, peruse relevant documents, inspect the site, etc.; provided, the ombudsman may request for the statement of opinions or explanation to the relevant employee, if perusal is impracticable because the relevant documents, etc. fall under Article 9(1)2 and 5 of the Official Information Disclosure Act. <Added on Mar. 31, 2010; Oct. 31, 2023>
  (9) No ombudsman shall concurrently hold any of the following office: <Added on Mar. 31, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. A member of the National Assembly or a member of a local council;
  2. A member of a political party, or a member of an association, the main purpose of which is to conduct political activities;
  3. An executive officer or employee of a defense contractor, general enterprise, general enterprise not related to the defense industry, specialized research institute, general research institute, or military supply sales agency.
  (10) Matters necessary for the operation of the ombudsman system referred to in paragraph (5), such as the composition of ombudsmen, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 31, 2010; Oct. 31, 2023>

Article 6-2 (Request for inquiry into criminal records for confirmation of revocation of designation of defense contractors) (1) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may request the heads of relevant agencies (including the heads of relevant military agencies; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) to inquire into criminal records in order to verify whether a person falling under Article 6(1)4 has committed a crime corresponding to a violation of the Military Secret Protection Act or the Defense Technology Security Act and falls under any of the following grounds:
  1. Revocation of bids or successful bids, and cancellation or termination of contracts under the main clause of Article 6(4);
  2. Revocation of designation of defense contractors under Article 48(1)1;
  3. Restrictions on qualification for participation in bidding under Article 59(1)6.
  (2) The head of a related agency in receipt of a request for inquiry into criminal records pursuant to paragraph (1) shall comply with such request, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures, scope, etc. of criminal record inquiries under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 16, 2024]

Article 7 (System of qualifications for position) (1) In order to enhance efficiency and professionalism in executing defense acquisition programs, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall appoint persons with corresponding qualifications to positions particularly deemed to require expertise.
  (2) The extent of position, standards for qualifications, and other necessary matters pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 8 (Research of legal issues regarding defense acquisition programs) In order to prevent any act that may inflict financial loss on the State in the execution of defense acquisition programs and to ensure the smooth execution of defense acquisition programs, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall proceed the matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as contracts, negotiations, etc., after having legal experts, etc. review legal issues, etc. thereof.

Article 9 (Defense Acquisition Program Promotion Committee) (1) To deliberate upon and coordinate major policies and operation of financial resources and others for the promotion of defense acquisition programs, the Defense Acquisition Program Promotion Committee shall be established under the control of the Minister of National Defense (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee").
  (2) The Committee shall deliberate upon and coordinate the following: <Amended on May 9, 2014; Feb. 4, 2020; Mar. 31, 2020; May 16, 2023; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. Matters regarding major policies and plans relating to defense acquisition programs;
  2. Matters regarding the formulation of medium-term plans in the fields of defense force improvement projects provided for in Article 13(3);
  3. Matters regarding the formulation of budgets for defense force improvement projects provided for in Article 14(1);
  4. Matters regarding the determination of the methods of implementing defense force improvement projects provided for in Article 17;
  5. Matters regarding the determination of types of weapons systems, equipment, etc. to be purchased;
  6. Matters regarding offset trade provided for in Article 20;
  7. Matters regarding the analysis, evaluation, and utilization of the outcomes thereof provided for in Articles 23 and 24;
  8. Matters regarding the standardization of military supplies and quality assurance provided for in Articles 26 and 28;
  9. Matters regarding contracts for the procurement of military supplies;
  10. Matters regarding the formulation of basic plans for defense science and technology innovation under Article 6(1) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  11. Matters regarding the formulation of basic plans for developing the defense industry provided for in Article 5 of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  11-2. Matters regarding the designation of national policy projects in the defense industry pursuant to Article 8 of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  12. Matters regarding the designation of defense materials under Article 34;
  13. Matters regarding the coordination of projects and request for measures provided for in Article 11 of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  14. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration to be deliberated upon and coordinated by the Committee.
  (3) The Committee shall be comprised of not exceeding 25 members, including one Chairperson. In such cases, the Committee shall include not more than four persons falling under paragraph (4)5 and three persons falling under paragraph (4)6, respectively. <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016; Nov. 28, 2017>
  (4) The Minister of National Defense shall become the Chairperson of the Committee, and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall become the Vice Chairperson of the Committee, and the following persons shall become its members: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; May 9, 2014; Mar. 21, 2017; Jul. 26, 2017; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The Vice Minister of National Defense;
  2. Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree, from among public officials of assistant ministerial or director general level, or general-level officers of the Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, and each military branch;
  3. Public officials in general service belonging to the Senior Executive Service of the Ministry of Science and ICT, the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Resources, or the Ministry of Planning and Budget, who are designated by the head of the relevant agency;
  4. The President of the Agency for Defense Development, the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality established under Article 32, and the President of the Korea Institute for Defense Analyses established under the Korea Institute for Defense Analyses Act;
  5. Persons entrusted by the Minister of National Defense, from among persons recommended by the relevant Standing Committee of the National Assembly;
  6. Persons entrusted by the Minister of National Defense, from among persons recommended by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, who have sufficient knowledge and experience in defense acquisition programs or are learned and venerable.
  (5) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Committee, terms of office of its members, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 10 (Subcommittees, working committees, and expert members) (1) In order to efficiently perform the business affairs of the Committee, subcommittees shall be established for each field.
  (2) Where the Committee deems it necessary, the Committee may further deliberate upon and coordinate the matters deliberated upon and coordinated by the subcommittees, which were delegated by the Committee or prescribed by Presidential Decree as concerning the subcommittees.
  (3) Each subcommittee may establish a working committee to assist the subcommittee in its affairs. <Added on Dec. 20, 2016>
  (4) The Chairperson of the Committee may appoint expert members, from among those with expertise and experience in defense acquisition programs, to seek advice on the major matters of deliberation by the Committee and subcommittees. <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016>
  (5) Expert members may attend and speak at the Committee and subcommittees, and may submit written opinions to the Committee, if necessary. <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016>
  (6) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of subcommittees and working committees, the term of office of expert members, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016>
  [Title Amended on Dec. 20, 2016]

CHAPTER III DEFENSE FORCE IMPROVEMENT PROJECTS

SECTION 1 Principles for Execution of Defense Force Improvement Projects

Article 11 (Basic principles for execution of defense force improvement projects) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration implements defense force improvement projects, he or she shall comply with the following principles:
  1. Conducting research and development, and promotion of localization of weapons systems for the accomplishment of self-reliant national defense through the development of national defense science and technology;
  2. Maximizing defense capability by acquiring weapons systems with optimal performance, as required by each service;
  3. Devising a stable comprehensive plan for the assistance of ordinances for the efficient operation of weapons systems;
  4. Securing transparency and specialization at all phases of the advancement of defense force improvement projects;
  5. Deleted; <Mar. 31, 2020>
  6. Deleted. <Mar. 31, 2020>

Article 12 (Comprehensive project administration system) (1) Where it is necessary for the efficient execution of defense force improvement projects, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall implement a comprehensive project administration system that administers all procedures of a unit project by having the person who administers a unit project comprehensively organize the specialized manpower by function, such as formulation of plans, drawing up of budgets, decisions on types of weapons, negotiation, administration of contracts, administration of quality assurance, administration of technology, etc.
  (2) Matters necessary for the method of operation, procedures for, etc. of the comprehensive project administration system pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1) shall be decided by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.

SECTION 2 Medium-Term National Defense Plan and Budget

Article 13 (Medium-term national defense plan) (1) The Minister of National Defense shall formulate a medium-term plan (hereinafter referred to as "medium-term national defense plan") with approval from the President regarding defense force improvement projects, military force management, etc. to build rational military force. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall prepare a written request for a medium-term plan for defense force improvement projects in consideration of the order of priority in the requirements for weapons systems, a national financial operating plan, etc., and submit it to the Minister of National Defense. In such cases, where the relevant Standing Committee of the National Assembly requests him or her to report on the written request for the medium-term plan, he or she shall report thereon. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (3) The Minister of National Defense shall formulate a medium-term national defense plan after receiving a request for a medium-term plan for defense force improvement projects from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, reviewing the adequacy of requirements for weapons systems, etc., and undergoing a prior deliberation and coordination by the Committee. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (4) Where the Minister of National Defense formulates a medium-term national defense plan, he or she shall immediately report the details thereof to the relevant Standing Committee of the National Assembly. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (5) Matters necessary for the formulation of a medium-term national defense plan, a requirement review, etc. referred to in paragraphs (1) and (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on May 9, 2014>

Article 14 (Budget formulation and execution) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall formulate a budget for defense force improvement projects, based on the medium-term national defense plan and the Minister of National Defense's guidelines for budget formulation, and report it to the Minister of National Defense.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall establish a budget execution plan and budget operating plan to efficiently execute and administer the budget for defense force improvement projects.
  (3) Matters necessary for budget formulation and execution referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 14-2 (Project feasibility studies) (1) In order to compile a budget for any of the following new large projects prescribed by Presidential Decree, the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall conduct a feasibility study by which matters related to the promotion of the project are transparently and fairly examined before the commencement of the project:
  1. A force support system project under subparagraph 4 of Article 3;
  2. A weapons system purchase project under Article 19;
  3. A defense research and development project under subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may exclude any of the following projects from projects subject to project feasibility studies in accordance with the procedures determined by the Minister of Planning and Budget: <Added on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Projects requiring extreme confidentiality in relation to national security;
  2. Projects for which urgent requirements for weapons systems, etc. are determined pursuant to the proviso of Article 17(1) and for which there are emergency circumstances, such as wartime, incidents, overseas deployment, infiltration or provocation of enemies, or terrorism;
  3. Other Projects for which project feasibility studies are deemed to have no practical benefit, such as the method of implementing the project or the project for which the budget calculation is obvious.
  (3) The Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall designate any of the following persons as an executing agency and have them conduct research related to the project feasibility study under paragraph (1); provided, where necessary to efficiently conduct the study, the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may change the executing agency or additionally designate an executing agency in consultation with the Minister of Planning and Budget: <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The Korea Institute for Defense Analyses established under the Korea Institute for Defense Analyses Act;
  2. The Korea Institute of Science and Technology Evaluation and Planning under the Framework Act on Science and Technology.
  (4) Projects subject to project feasibility studies, criteria for selection thereof, and methods and procedures for such studies under paragraph (1) and the designation, etc. of executing agencies under paragraph (2) shall be determined by the Minister of Economy and Finance. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  (5) Projects subject to project feasibility studies, criteria for selection thereof, and methods and procedures for such studies under paragraph (1) and the designation, etc. of executing agencies under paragraph (2) shall be determined by the Minister of Planning and Budget. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Feb. 3, 2022]

SECTION 3 Decision on Requirements and Revision Thereof

Article 15 (Decision on requirements) (1) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall determine the requirements for weapons systems, etc. following deliberation by the Joint Chiefs of Staff on the requirements for defense force improvement projects raised by each service branch, military units under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense, and related agencies, and where the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff recognizes that the requirements are appropriate after examining the jointness, redundancy of force requirements, scale of projects, etc., the Chiefs of Staff of each service branch and the Commandant of the Marine Corps may determine the requirements. In such cases, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall hear the opinions of the Commissioner of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, and shall gather opinions from civilian experts and interested persons, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 9, 2014; Nov. 28, 2017; Feb. 6, 2024>
  (2) To ensure that decisions on requirements referred to in paragraph (1) can be made objectively and rationally, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall organize human resources performing related duties in a way that the three services of the armed forces are balanced. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (3) Procedures, etc. for decisions on requirements referred to in paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 9, 2014>

Article 15-2 (Decision on expeditious requirements) (1) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall, if considered necessary to rapidly implement force integration of weapons systems applying the advanced technology in the private sector, determine expeditious requirements, after deliberation by the Joint Chiefs of Staff. In such cases, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall consider the opinions of the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, and when expeditious requirements are determined, he or she shall report the decision to the competent standing committee of the National Assembly without delay.
  (2) Procedures for decision-making on expeditious requirements under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 16, 2023]

Article 15-3 (Conducting prior conceptual studies) (1) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff may require the Agency for Defense Development to conduct research on the necessity of weapons systems, etc., operational concept, operational performance, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "prior conceptual studies") so as to ensure the expeditious and reasonable decision on requirements under Article 15(1) or 15-2(1); provided, where it is necessary for effective research, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff may change or additionally designate implementing agencies in consultation with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may subsidize all or part of the expenses incurred in conducting prior conceptual studies within the budget.
  (3) The procedures for conducting prior conceptual studies shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 16, 2023]

Article 16 (Revision to requirements) (1) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff may revise the requirements for weapons systems, etc. determined under Article 15(1) or 15-2(1) after deliberation by the Council of the Joint Chiefs of Staff; provided, where the Chief of Staff of each service branch and the Commander of the Marine Corps determine the requirements under Article 15(1), the requirements may be revised after self- deliberation by the relevant service branch in consultation with the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff. <Amended on May 9, 2014; May 16, 2023; Feb. 6, 2024>
  (2) Where the requirements for weapons systems, etc. are revised pursuant to paragraph (1), Article 15(1) or 15-2(1) shall apply mutatis mutandis; provided, this shall not apply to modification of any minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 16, 2023>

SECTION 4 Execution of Defense Force Improvement Projects

Article 17 (Methods of implementing defense force improvement projects) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall, in the process of determining requirements for weapons systems, etc. for defense force improvement projects under Article 15(1), conduct prior research to investigate and analyze the feasibility of research and development, the timing and quantity of requirements, the level of national defense science and technology, the effects of fostering the defense industry, technical and economic feasibility, cost-effectiveness (including life-cycle costs incurred from the acquisition of weapons systems through their operation and maintenance), etc. with respect to the relevant weapons systems, and, where the requirements are determined, shall determine the method for implementing the defense force improvement project; provided, where there are urgent requirements for weapons systems, etc. for a defense force improvement project due to wartime, armed conflict, overseas deployment, etc., or where a pilot project is conducted under Article 17-2(1), prior research need not be conducted in the process of determining the requirements; and where, after conducting a pilot project under that paragraph, the Administrator determines the method for implementing the defense force improvement project without conducting prior research, the Administrator shall report such determination to the competent Standing Committee of the National Assembly without delay. <Amended on May 16, 2023; Jan. 16, 2024; Dec. 30, 2025>
  (2) Where it is necessary to conduct the prior research pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1), the opinions of the Agency for Defense Development, each service, the relevant Ministry, etc. shall be reflected.
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraphs (1) and (2), where a defense capability improvement project promoted by the Defense Acquisition Program Administration is a space development project jointly carried out with the head of another central administrative agency, it may be carried out in accordance with the procedures for national research and development projects under the Act on the Innovation of National Research and Development and the procedures for carrying out space development under the Space Development Promotion Act; provided, with respect to testing and evaluation, standardization, quality assurance, etc. not prescribed in the Act on the Innovation of National Research and Development or the Space Development Promotion Act, the provisions on procedures for defense capability improvement projects may be applied. <Added on Mar. 18, 2025>
  (4) The methods of implementing defense force improvement projects pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be classified into purchasing area under Article 19 or research and development area under Article 8 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act. <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020; Mar. 18, 2025>

Article 17-2 (Conducting pilot projects) (1) For the purpose of utilization of new technology specified in subparagraph 5d of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Chiefs of Staff of each service branch, and the Commander of the Marine Corps may implement pilot projects for weapons systems, etc., the requirements of which have not been determined under Article 15(1) or 15-2(1).
  (2) In order to objectively and reasonably evaluate the outcomes of a pilot project referred to in paragraph (1), the Chiefs of Staff of each service branch and the Commander of the Marine Corps may organize and operate a performance test team consisting of public officials and employees of the agencies specified in subparagraphs 1 and 2, and civilian experts referred to in subparagraph 3:
  1. The Ministry of National Defense, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, and each service of each service branch;
  2. The Agency for Defense Development, and the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality established pursuant to Article 32;
  3. Civilian experts referred to in Article 21(6).
  (3) Procedures for pilot projects under paragraph (1) and procedures for composition and operation of the performance test team under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 16, 2023]

Article 18 Deleted. <Mar. 31, 2020>

Article 19 (Purchase) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall preferentially purchase domestically-manufactured munitions; provided, he or she may purchase the munitions manufactured overseas where it is difficult to purchase them in Korea.
  (2) Where it is necessary for the efficient execution of purchasing business, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may have the civilian specialists prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as persons, etc. recommended by the Committee, who have a track record of working in the field of international contracting, participate in the purchase procedures.
  (3) Matters necessary for purchase procedures, etc. for the promotion of defense force improvement projects shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 20 (Offset trade) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration purchases military supplies from overseas under Article 19(1) (including where military supplies are purchased by concluding a contract with a foreign government), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall, in principle, promote offset trade for unit projects of at least the amount prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 16, 2024>
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration selects technology, etc. that can be secured through offset trade, he or she shall have it linked with basic plans and implementation plans for defense science and technology innovation under Article 6 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act. <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020>
  (3) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to promote offset trade, he or she shall satisfy any of the following conditions: <Amended on Apr. 1, 2009>
  1. Securing technology necessary for defense force improvement projects;
  2. Securing logistic support capability for the purchase of weapons systems;
  3. Participation in the development and manufacture of weapons systems manufactured in the country of the contracting party;
  4. The export of military supplies, such as defense materials, etc.;
  5. Securing maintenance materials for the weapons systems from the country of the contracting party;
  6. Implementation of matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as export linkages for commodities other than military supplies (limited to cases where the conditions under any of subparagraphs 1 through 5 are satisfied).

Article 21 (Test and evaluation) (1) The Minister of National Defense shall formulate a test and evaluation plan including standards, items, methods, timing, etc. of evaluation for the test and evaluation of weapons systems and core technologies. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (2) Each armed forces and each agency (referring to the Agency for Defense Development, defense contractors, general enterprises, specialized research institutes, and general research institutes; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) shall conduct tests and evaluations of weapons systems and core technologies in accordance with the test and evaluation plans under paragraph (1). In such cases, the outcome of tests and evaluations shall be notified to the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (3) The test and evaluation on research and development among the tests and evaluations of weapons systems and core technologies under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be conducted separately according to classification into the developmental test and evaluation and the operational test and evaluation; provided, where it is determined as expeditious requirements pursuant to Article 15-2(1), the developmental test and evaluation and the operational test may be conducted in combination: <Added on Jan. 19, 2016; May 16, 2023>
  1. Developmental test and evaluation: Test and evaluation to verify whether pilot products of equipment developed satisfy required performance, development goals, etc.;
  2. Operational test and evaluation: Test and evaluation to confirm whether pilot products of equipment developed satisfy required operational capability and are appropriate for the military operations.
  (4) For test and evaluation for purchasing weapons systems among the tests and evaluations of weapons systems under paragraphs (1) and (2), each service branch and each agency may conduct test and evaluation under any of the following or by complementing the following ones. In such cases, in principle, test and evaluation shall be conducted by the method specified in subparagraph 1, but in cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as being developing weapons systems, test and evaluation may be conducted by the method specified in subparagraph 2: <Added on Jan. 19, 2016>
  1. Test and evaluation based on the real thing: Test and evaluation of weapons systems or pilot products, the development of which has been completed;
  2. Test and evaluation based on data: Test and evaluation based on data relating to proposed performance presented by an enterprise.
  (5) Where the Minister of National Defense purchases a weapons system that has undergone a pilot project pursuant to Article 17-2(1), he or she may replace the test and evaluation referred to in paragraph (4) with the results of performance test. <Added on May 16, 2023>
  (6) Where it is necessary for the enhancement of professionalism and transparency of test and evaluation, the Minister of National Defense may have civilian experts participate in test and evaluation upon recommendation by the Committee. <Amended on May 9, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; May 16, 2023>
  (7) The Minister of National Defense shall judge whether weapons systems and core technologies satisfy the standards, etc. of test and evaluation on the grounds of the outcomes of test and evaluation notified pursuant to paragraph (2), and report the judgment to the Committee. <Amended on May 9, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; May 16, 2023>
  (8) Other matters necessary for the formulation of the test and evaluation plans and the methods and procedures for the test and evaluation shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; May 16, 2023>

Article 21-2 (Support for test and evaluation) (1) The Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may designate the Agency for Defense Development and the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality established under Article 32 as test and evaluation support bodies that perform the following tasks, in order to provide technical support for test and evaluation under Article 21:
  1. Technical support, such as the analysis of test and evaluation results;
  2. Management of test and evaluation data;
  3. Research on test and evaluation techniques;
  4. Technical support necessary to secure test facilities and test equipment required to perform tasks under subparagraph 5a and b of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  5. Technical support for international cooperation regarding the utilization of test facilities and test equipment;
  6. Other tasks prescribed by Presidential Decree for supporting test and evaluation, etc.
  (2) The Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may contribute all or part of the expenses incurred in providing support for test and evaluation, etc. under paragraph (1), within the budget.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 10, 2026]

Article 22 (Improvement of performance) (1) In order to enhance the performance and quality of the weapons systems in operation or in the manufacturing process, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may promote the improvement of performance such weapon systems.
  (2) Where the surroundings of operation of weapons systems change substantially or the important operation performance of the weapons systems changes notwithstanding the provisions of paragraph (1), the improvement of performance shall be promoted in accordance with the procedures for decisions on requirements pursuant to Article 15.
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures for promotion, etc. of improvement of performance pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.

SECTION 5 Analysis and Evaluation

Article 23 (Conduct of analysis and evaluation) (1) To seek rational decision-making and use financial resources effectively in executing defense force improvement projects, the system of analysis and evaluation of defense force improvement projects shall be established and the analysis and evaluation shall be conducted accordingly. <Amended on Mar. 31, 2010>
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall conduct the analysis and evaluation of the following matters: <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  1. The analysis and evaluation necessary for the preparation of a written request for a medium-term plan, formulation of a budget, etc. in the field of defense force improvement projects before the execution of the budget of the relevant project;
  2. The analysis and evaluation of interim outcomes, etc. of the project in the course of executing the budget of the relevant project;
  3. The analysis and evaluation of the outcomes of conducting the project, etc. after the completion of implementation of budget of the relevant project.
  (3) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraph (2), the Minister of National Defense shall analyze and evaluate decisions on requirements for defense force improvement projects, formulate a medium-term plan, field weapons systems, etc. In such cases, the Minister may assign the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff or the Chief of Staff of each service to perform such duties. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (4) The Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may have civilian specialists participate in analysis and evaluation if necessary to enhance the credibility of analysis and evaluation.
  (5) The methods, procedures, etc. of analysis and evaluation referred to in paragraphs (2) through (4) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.

Article 24 (Utilization of outcomes of analysis and evaluation) (1) In order to efficiently execute defense force improvement projects, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall arrange for the outcomes of the analysis and evaluation pursuant to the provisions of Article 23(2)1 and 2 to be utilized in the decision making of each phase of promotion of the relevant defense force improvement projects.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall arrange for the outcomes of the analysis and evaluation pursuant to the provisions of Article 23(2)3 to be utilized in the decision making for the relevant defense force improvement projects.
  (3) The Minister of National Defense shall arrange for the outcomes of the analysis and evaluation pursuant to the provisions of Article 23(3) to be utilized in the decisions, etc. on requirements for defense force improvement projects, and may request the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration to re-analyze or re-evaluate the outcomes of the analysis and evaluation referred to in Article 23(2) or take corrective measures, if necessary in consideration of the surroundings of operations of armed forces, technological changes, etc.

CHAPTER IV PROCUREMENT AND QUALITY ASSURANCE

Article 25 (Planning and methods of procurement) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall formulate a plan for procuring munitions in accordance with the guidance of the Minister of National Defense, and procure munitions accordingly.
  (2) In order to efficiently implement the defense budget, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall procure munitions en bloc; provided, each service may procure directly, or request the Public Procurement Service to make purchases as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 26 (Standardization) (1) In order to efficiently acquire munitions, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall formulate a plan for the standardization of munitions for an efficient acquisition of munitions. In this case, the Korean Industrial Standard under Article 12 of the Industrial Standardization Act shall be reflected on the matters to the extent applicable. <Amended on May 25, 2007>
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall designate or revoke standard articles in accordance with the plan formulated pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1), and formulate, amend, or abolish the standards for munitions, and administer munitions by distinguishing their physical or functional characteristics.
  (3) Matters necessary for the designation or revocation of standard articles in accordance with paragraph (2), and the formulation, amendment, or abolition of standards for munitions, and their administration in accordance with the physical or functional characteristics of munitions shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 27 (Military supply catalog information) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall administer the munitions inventory information by classifying the munitions in accordance with the standardization pursuant to the provisions of Article 26, by designating names and stock numbers, and by logging their characteristics, etc.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall formulate and execute a plan for the administration and utilization of information on inventory of munitions pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1), and endeavor to engage in the international exchange of munitions inventory information.

Article 28 (Quality assurance) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall formulate and execute a plan for quality assurance including measures to modify and supplement inadequacies in accordance with the examination of the quality of the munitions in order to verify whether they conform to the initial requirements of the user at each stage of research and development, and purchasing.
  (2) The concrete methods, etc. regarding quality assurance at each stage pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.

Article 28-2 (Definition of counterfeit parts and prohibition of handling) (1) "Counterfeit parts, etc." in this Act means any of the following goods used for a contract under this Act, which falls under any of the following:
  1. Goods infringing on trademark rights under Article 108 of the Trademark Act;
  2. Goods constituting an act of unfair competition defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Unfair Competition Prevention and Trade Secret Protection Act;
  3. Goods violating Article 33 of the Foreign Trade Act;
  4. Goods violating Article 38 of the Foreign Trade Act.
  (2) No one shall produce, manufacture, or process counterfeit parts, etc. referred to in paragraph (1), or import, sell, or use counterfeit parts, etc. while knowing that they are counterfeit parts, etc.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 16, 2024]

Article 29 (Quality control) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall, after consultation with the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources, establish standards for responsibility management, resources management, etc. by defense contractors or specialized research institutes to assure quality in the manufacture of defense materials. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Defense contractors or specialized research institutes shall take measures necessary for the quality control of defense materials in accordance with the standards referred to in paragraph (1).
  (3) Where necessary to conduct research and development, purchase and manufacture of defense materials, the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may, after consultation among themselves, have the defense contractors or specialized research institutes report thereon, or dispatch the relevant public officials, etc. to the facilities of the defense industry (hereinafter referred to as "defense industry facilities") or other necessary places to provide instructions on quality control or technology. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) The relevant public officials, etc. dispatched pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (3) may request the defense contractors or the managers of specialized research institutes to take necessary measures for the quality control, etc. of defense materials.

Article 29-2 (Certification of quality management system) (1) Where any of the following enterprises (hereinafter referred to as "defense contractor, etc.") has constructed a quality management system that may assure the quality of military supplies in accordance with standards for certification of the quality management system (hereinafter referred to as "standards for certification of the quality management system") prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may provide certification of the quality management system to such defense contractor, etc.:
  1. Defense contractors;
  2. General enterprises;
  3. General enterprises which have no relationship with the defense industry (limited to where they supply military supplies in accordance with standards for military supplies under Article 26(2)).
  (2) A defense contractor, etc. that intends to receive certification of quality management shall file an application therefor with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (3) The effective period of certification of quality management shall be four years from the date a defense contractor, etc. receives the certification thereof, and where the defense contractor, etc. that has received certification of quality management intends to maintain the validity of the certification thereof, it shall renew the certification thereof before the effective period thereof expires. <Amended on Feb. 6, 2024>
  (4) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may examine whether a defense contractor, etc. that has received certification of quality management meets standards for certification of quality management (hereinafter referred to as "examination of post management") during the effective period under paragraph (3), and where he or she does not deem that the result of the examination thereof meets standards for certification of quality management, he or she may order the defense contractor, etc. to take measures necessary for correction.
  (5) Matters necessary for methods, procedures, etc. for filing applications for, conducting examination, renewal, examination of the post management, etc. of certification of quality management shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 20, 2016]

Article 29-3 (Revocation of certification of quality management) Where a defense contractor, etc. that has received certification of quality management falls under any of the following cases, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may revoke the certification thereof; provided, where the defense contractor, etc. falls under subparagraph 1, he or she shall revoke the certification thereof:
  1. Where the defense contractor, etc. receives certification of quality management by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where the defense contractor, etc. fails to meet standards for certification of quality management;
  3. Where the defense contractor, etc. fails to undergo examination of post management without just grounds or fails to comply with an order to take corrective measures as a result of the examination thereof;
  4. Where it is deemed impracticable for the defense contractor, etc. to produce defense materials, etc. due to the cessation of business, etc.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 20, 2016]

Article 29-4 (Offering incentives to certified enterprises) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration enters into a contract for the procurement of military supplies or research and development of defense materials with a defense contractor, etc. that has received certification of quality management, he or she may offer incentives, such as awarding extra points, to the defense contractor, etc.
  (2) Matters necessary for the details of, methods, procedures, etc. for offering incentives under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 20, 2016]

CHAPTER V ADVANCEMENT OF NATIONAL DEFENSE SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY

Article 30 Deleted. <Mar. 31, 2020>

Article 31 Deleted. <Mar. 31, 2020>

Article 31-2 Deleted. <Mar. 31, 2020>

Article 32 (Defense Agency for Technology and Quality) (1) The Defense Agency for Technology and Quality shall be established to efficiently conduct the business of securing, distribution, administration, quality assurance, etc. of information regarding national defense science and technology, and munitions. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (2) The Defense Agency for Technology and Quality shall be established as a juristic person.
  (3) The Defense Agency for Technology and Quality shall come into being by registering the incorporation thereof at the location of its principal office.
  (4) The articles of incorporation of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality shall contain the following matters:
  1. Objectives;
  2. Name;
  3. The location of its principal office;
  4. Matters regarding business and finance;
  5. Matters regarding officers;
  6. Matters regarding the board of directors;
  7. Matters regarding the amendment of the articles of incorporation;
  8. Matters regarding its dissolution.
  (5) Where the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality intends to draft or amend the articles of incorporation, it shall obtain authorization therefor from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. In such cases, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall obtain approval therefor from the Minster of National Defense before granting authorization.
  (6) The Defense Agency for Technology and Quality shall conduct the following business activities: <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020; Mar. 31, 2020; Jun. 20, 2023>
  1. Deleted; <Mar. 31, 2020>
  2. Deleted; <Mar. 31, 2020>
  3. Deleted; <Mar. 31, 2020>
  4. Deleted; <Mar. 31, 2020>
  5. Business support for the quality assurance of military supplies, quality control of defense materials, etc. and the relevant business entrusted by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration;
  6. Analysis, evaluation, and research on the reliability for the lifespan, durability, etc. of military supplies;
  7. Performance of tests for guarantee of quality, etc. of military supplies;
  8. Business support for the standardization, etc. of military supplies requested in the course of execution of defense acquisition programs;
  9. Matters regarding the collection and supply of information on import prices of military supplies;
  10. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree with regard to the quality control, etc. of military supplies.
  (7) The Government shall contribute expenses necessary for the establishment and operation of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality.
  (8) Matters necessary for the operation, supervision, etc. of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (9) The provisions in the Civil Act regarding incorporated foundations shall apply mutatis mutandis to matters not prescribed in this Act regarding the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality.

Article 32-2 (Special cases concerning permission to use military supplies, State-owned property, and public property) (1) Where necessary for the operation of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, the State or a local government may permit the Agency to use, or may lease or transfer to it, free of charge, military supplies, State-owned property, or public property, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, notwithstanding the Act on the Management of Military Supplies, the State Property Act, or the Public Property and Commodity Management Act. <Amended on Dec. 30, 2025>
  (2) Where State-owned property or public property is permitted to be used or leased under paragraph (1), the period thereof may be up to 20 years, notwithstanding Articles 35(1) and 46(1) of the State Property Act or Articles 21(1) and 31(1) of the Public Property and Commodity Management Act. In such cases, the period may be renewed. <Added on Dec. 30, 2025>
  (3) Where the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality has obtained permission to use or a lease of land owned by the State or a local government under paragraph (1), it may construct permanent facilities on such land, notwithstanding the State Property Act or the Public Property and Commodity Management Act. In such cases, taking into account the type, etc. of such facilities, a condition may be imposed that, upon expiration of the period, such facilities be donated to the State or the local government or that the land be restored to its original condition and returned. <Added on Dec. 30, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Sep. 1, 2015]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 30, 2025]

Article 32-3 (Special cases concerning national defense and military installations projects) The Defense Agency for Technology and Quality may implement a national defense and military installations project in accordance with the Act on National Defense and Military Installations Projects. In such cases, the Agency shall be deemed a person implementing a national defense and military installations project under other statutes, as referred to in subparagraph 3 of Article 3 of that Act.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 30, 2025]

CHAPTER VI FOSTERING OF DEFENSE INDUSTRY

Article 33 Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 34 (Designation of defense materials) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may, after consultation with the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources, designate materials classified as weapons systems as defense materials, which are necessary for the securing of stable source of procurement, strict quality assurance, etc.; provided, materials that are not classified as weapons systems but prescribed by Presidential Decree may be designated as defense materials. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Defense materials shall be designated by classifying them into either major defense materials or general defense materials.
  (3) The classification into either major defense materials or general defense materials pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (2), and other matters necessary for the designation of defense materials shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 35 (Designation of defense contractors) (1) Those who intend to manufacture defense materials shall obtain designation as defense contractors from the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources after meeting the standards for facilities, the requirements of security, etc. that are prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources shall have a prior consultation with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration regarding the designation of defense contractors. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources designates a defense contractor pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall designate by classifying the contractor as either a major defense contractor or general defense contractor. Enterprises which manufacture any of the following defense materials shall be major defense contractors, and enterprises that manufacture other defense materials shall be general defense contractors: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Firearms and other fire power weapons;
  2. Guided weapons;
  3. Aircraft;
  4. Vessels;
  5. Ammunition;
  6. Tanks, armored vehicles, and other mobile combat equipment;
  7. Radars, identification friend or foe, and other communication and electronic equipment;
  8. Night observation devices and other optical or thermal imaging devices;
  9. Combat engineering equipment;
  10. Chemical, biological and radiological warfare equipment;
  11. Command and control systems;
  12. Other materials that the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration designates as recognized to be important for military strategy or tactical operations.
  (3) Where the change in substance of the governance of management of a defense contractor because of transaction and public sale, or merger and acquisition, or other reasons is expected and it falls under the standards prescribed by Presidential Decree, those who intend to gain a controlling interest in the management of the relevant defense contractor shall submit relevant documents to the Minister of Trade, and Industry as prescribed by Presidential Decree and obtain his or her prior approval therefor; provided, this shall not apply where permission from the Minister of Trade, Industry and Energy has been obtained pursuant to Article 6(1) through (4) of the Foreign Investment Promotion Act. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 27, 2016; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Where the Minister of Trade, and Industry intends to grant approval pursuant to the main clause of paragraph (3), he or she shall have a prior consultation with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) Matters necessary for designation pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 36 Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 37 (Protection and fosterage) (1) The Government shall guarantee defense materials manufactured and procured by defense contractors.
  (2) The Government shall provide priority to defense contractors who manufacture major defense materials when assisting in the following matters: <Amended on Apr. 1, 2009; Feb. 4, 2020; Mar. 31, 2020>
  1. Conducting defense research and development projects under Article 8(1) and (4) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  2. The loan of funds under Article 12(1) of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  3. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree for the protection and fosterage of defense contractors.

Article 38 Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 39 Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 40 Deleted. <Mar. 31, 2020>

Article 41 (Assistance to defense industry) (1) For the purposes of research and development, or the manufacture of defense materials, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, each service branch, the Agency for Defense Development, the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, and military repair units may give technical assistance or assistance in manufacturing to defense contractors or specialized research institutes at the expense of the defense contractors or specialized research institutes. <Amended on Mar. 21, 2017>
  (2) Where the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deem necessary for the research and development of weapons systems and technologies related thereto of defense contractors, general enterprises, specialized research institutes, and general research institutes, they may allow defense contractors, general enterprises, specialized research institutes, and general research institutes to jointly utilize facilities, equipment, and information related to test and evaluation of the Agency for Defense Development in accordance with methods and procedures prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Added on Mar. 21, 2017>
  (3) Where a defense contractor intends to produce defense materials or to modify or develop defense materials at its own expense for the promotion of exports or for national defense research and development and to possess or dispose of such defense materials, it shall obtain approval from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Added on Jul. 22, 2025>
  (4) The requirements and procedures for approval under paragraph (3), the procedures for the management and disposal of defense materials in possession, and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Jul. 22, 2025>

Article 42 Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 43 (Designation of guarantee organizations) (1) In order to reduce the financial burden of defense contractors, etc. and to allow the defense contractors, etc. to use the guarantee organization conveniently, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may designate an organization (hereinafter referred to as "guarantee organization") that performs guarantee business pursuant to the provisions of any subparagraphs in paragraph (2) relating to defense business.
  (2) The scope of guarantee business of a guarantee organization shall be as listed in the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Apr. 1, 2009; Feb. 4, 2020; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. Payment guarantees against loans under Article 12(1) of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  2. Payment guarantees against bid bonds, contract deposits, and defects liability bonds against the procurement and research of defense materials and the contract for manufacturing of prototypes;
  3. Payment guarantees against a retaining fee and progress payments pursuant to the provisions of Article 46-2(1);
  4. Payment guarantees against government-supplied military supplies under the provisions of Article 24 of the Act on the Management of Military Supplies;
  5. Loan guarantees against funds necessary for reserving raw materials pursuant to the provisions of Article 55;
  6. Other guarantees necessary for defense contractors, etc. to conduct defense business.
  (3) Matters necessary for the requirements for designation, methods of designation, procedures of designation, etc. of guarantee organizations shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 44 Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>

Article 45 (Transfer or lease of State-owned property) (1) The Government may sell, lease, or permit the use of the State-owned property and goods (including military supplies; hereinafter the same shall apply) necessary for the performance of the defense industry in accordance with the following classification, notwithstanding the State Property Act, the Commodity Management Act, and the Act on the Management of Military Supplies: <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  1. General property and goods may be sold, or leased free of charge or at a cost, to defense contractors by private contract;
  2. Administrative property may be permitted for free use, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) If necessary to produce, research, and produce prototypes of defense materials, the Government may lease, or transfer, equipment for exclusive use or goods to defense contractors or specialized research institutes free of charge or at a cost, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, notwithstanding the Commodity Management Act. <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  (3) No defense contractors or specialized research institutes shall use the State-owned property or goods transferred, leased, or permitted for use, free of charge or at a cost pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2) for another purpose. <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  (4) Where defense contractors request the Government to transfer, lease, permit the use of, exchange, etc. defense facilities or defense materials owned by the State for exportation, the Government may transfer, lease, or permit the use of the defense facilities or defense materials free of charge or at a cost, or exchange them with defense materials owned by defense contractors, as long as they do not impede military operations or the maintenance of war potential, notwithstanding the provisions of other statutes or regulations. <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  (5) When a price difference exists in defense facilities or defense materials exchanged pursuant to paragraph (4), the difference shall be settled in cash.
  (6) Where the head of a central administrative agency intends to transfer the State-owned defense facilities referred to in paragraph (4), he or she shall first consult with the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Added on Mar. 27, 2015; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (7) Matters necessary for the transfer, lease, permit for use, exchange, etc. of defense facilities or defense materials provided for in paragraphs (4) and (5), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  [Title Amended on Mar. 27, 2015]

Article 46 (Special cases of contract) (1) The Government may conclude a defense acquisition contract by means of a short-term contract, long-term contract, final contract, estimated contract, etc. In such cases, Article 46 and 46-2 through 46-5 shall take precedence over other statutes, and the types, details, and methods of a defense acquisition contract and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (2) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall apply to matters regarding defense acquisition contracts under paragraph (1). <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (3) In a competitive bidding procedure for the conclusion of a defense acquisition contract, a successful bidder may be determined in accordance with the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree in consideration of the special characteristics, nature, size, etc. of the contract. <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (4) Where it is necessary to determine the estimated price in concluding a defense acquisition contract, the standards and method for determining the estimated price shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (5) Where a defense acquisition contract is concluded by rough estimate, the contract amount upon the settlement of accounts shall be determined based on the prime cost actually incurred. In such cases, the guidelines for, and the scope of, the settlement of accounts for the contract by rough estimate shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (6) Where the Government enters into a defense acquisition contract with the aim of establishment of national security, it may preferentially acquire raw materials, materials, components, products, etc. domestically produced as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Oct. 31, 2023>
  (7) Where it is necessary for purchasing or supplying a force support system that is to be used by the Ministry of National Defense and military units or organizations under the direct control of the Ministry, or by each service branch, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may conclude a unit price contract, which has at least two counter-parties to the contract who supply items with the same or similar quality or performance, etc., as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Jun. 11, 2014; May 16, 2023; Oct. 31, 2023>
  (8) If it is necessary for conducting a pilot project pursuant to Article 17-2(1), the Minister of Defense Acquisition Program Administration may conclude a contract with at least two counter-parties to the contract. <Added on May 16, 2023; Oct. 31, 2023>

Article 46-2 (Retaining fee and progress payments) (1) If it is deemed necessary to pay a retaining fee and progress payments to facilitate the performance of a defense acquisition contract, such retaining fee and progress payments may be paid to the extent appropriated in the budget for the relevant year. In such cases, the amount paid shall not be used for any purpose other than for performing the relevant contract.
  (2) With regard to a retaining fee and progress payments to be paid when a long-term contract is concluded under Article 46(1), the settlement of accounts may be postponed until the last delivery of goods subject to the contract.
  (3) Even where the counter-party to a defense acquisition contract is restricted from having qualification for participation in bidding procedures by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration pursuant to Article 59(1) of this Act or Article 27(1) of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may provide a retaining fee and progress payments as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to facilitate the performance of the defense acquisition contract concluded before the qualification for participation in bidding procedures is restricted; provided, such retaining fee and progress payments may be commensurate with the type, severity, etc. of grounds for the restrictions on the qualification for participation in bidding procedures, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Standards, methods, procedures, etc. for paying a retaining fee and progress payments under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 31, 2023]

Article 46-3 (Incentives for application of core technology) (1) Where a bidder applies to the object of a contract the core technology, future-leading defense technology, or new technology, etc. referred to in subparagraph 5b through d of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may grants such bidder incentives, such as additional points, when determining a successful bidder.
  (2) Matters necessary for the details of incentives, the criteria, methods, procedures, etc. of granting incentives under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 31, 2023]

Article 46-4 (Imposition of, reduction of, and exemption from compensation for deferment) (1) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall impose a compensation for deferment on the counter-party to a defense acquisition contract who has delayed the performance of a defense acquisition contract without good cause.
  (2) Where the performance of a contract is delayed due to any of the following grounds, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may fully or partially reduce or exempt a compensation for deferment:
  1. Where such delay occurs due to any of the grounds not attributable to the counter-party to a defense acquisition contract, which are prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. Where a counter-party to a defense acquisition contract, which is a national defense research and development contract under subparagraph 15a of Article 3 requiring a high level of technology, is deemed to have faithfully performed the contract;
  3. Where it is deemed inappropriate to impose all of the compensation for deferment on the counter-party to a defense acquisition contract, which is imposed because of delayed performance of the contract due to any of the following grounds:
  a. Where the cause of delay is concurrently attributable to the counter-party to a defense acquisition contract, the Government or subcontractor;
  b. Where the cause of delay is attributable only to the subcontractor;
  c. Where the cause of delay is resulting from severe testing conditions.
  (3) Matters necessary for criteria, methods, procedures, etc. for the imposition of and reduction of or exemption from a compensation for deferment under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 31, 2023]

Article 46-5 (Change of contract) An original defense acquisition contract which has been concluded on the premise that both parties to the contract agree on equal terms and will perform the contract according to the principle of good faith shall not be changed in principle; provided, the term, amount, conditions, etc. of the contract may be changed, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party and relevant statutes and regulations allow such change in the contract;
  2. Where it is a national defense research and development contract under subparagraph 15a of Article 3 requiring a high level of technology, the purpose of which is deemed unattainable even if the counter-party to the defense acquisition contract faithfully performs the contract;
  3. Where it is deemed impossible to achieve the purpose of the contract with the original contract amount unless the contract amount is adjusted, due to price fluctuation.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 31, 2023]

Article 47 (Grounds for disqualification from designation as defense contractor) None of the following persons shall be designated as a defense contractor:
  1. Where the officer (including spouse and direct ascendant or descendant) of a defense contractor, whose designation has been revoked pursuant to the provisions of Article 48(1), is an officer of another defense contractor that seeks to be designated before three years have passed since such revocation;
  2. Where a defense contractor seeks to be designated at the same place with the same facilities before six months have passed since the revocation of designation as a defense contractor pursuant to the provisions of Article 48(1).

Article 48 (Revocation of designation) (1) Where a defense contractor falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Trade, and Industry may revoke such designation after consultation with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Apr. 1, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; May 9, 2014; Dec. 20, 2016; Feb. 4, 2020; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where the representative or officer of a defense contractor violates the terms of a pledge of integrity provided for in Article 6;
  2. Where a defense contractor fails to meet the standards for facilities and requirements for security provided for in Article 35(1);
  3. Where a defense contractor fails to obtain approval provided for in Article 35(3);
  4. Where a defense contractor refuses, evades, or avoids performing the contract for supply of defense materials to the Government without just grounds;
  5. Where a defense contractor fails to comply with an order for performance provided for in Article 11(5) of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  6. Where a defense contractor obtains loans provided for in Article 12(1) of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act by fraud or other improper means, or uses loans for other purposes;
  7. Where a defense contractor obtains subsidies provided for in Article 13(1) of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act by fraud or other improper means, or used subsidies for other purposes;
  8. Where a defense contractor disposes of assets without obtaining approval provided for in Article 13(2) of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act;
  9. Where a defense contractor uses State assets or goods for other purposes in violation of Article 45(3);
  10. Where a defense contractor fails to comply with an order to take measures necessary for the replacement, supplementation, expansion, or relocation of facilities provided for in Article 49(1);
  11. Where a defense contractor violates an order provided for in Article 53(1);
  12. Where a defense contractor concludes a contract for supply by furnishing the Government with false or other unlawful cost data;
  13. Where a defense contractor employs a person subject to examination for fitness for employment, whose employment is restricted or who has not obtained approval for employment, in violation of Article 59-2(2);
  14. Where a defense contractor requests the revocation of designation as defense contractor from the Minister of Trade, and Industry along with the relevant documents as it has become incapable of conducting normal business due to nonpayment, bankruptcy, or other unavoidable operational reasons.
  (2) Where a defense contractor falls under any of paragraph (1)1 through 12, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may request the Minister of Trade, and Industry to revoke its designation. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where defense materials fall under any of the following cases, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may revoke the designation after consultation with the Minister of Trade, and Industry: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where it is recognized that it can be procured from at least two enterprises and the quality shall be guaranteed;
  2. Where there is no requirement for such materials from the military, or the system equipment has been deleted;
  3. Where military secrets are no longer requested pursuant to Article 2 of the Military Secret Protection Act as the grade of classification has lowered;
  4. Where the revocation of designation of defense materials is necessary, or there is no need to keep the designation of such defense materials following research and development, change, cancellation of purchase, etc.
  (4) Where a guarantee organization is engaged in business other than as stipulated in its articles of incorporation, or performs an act in violation of the conditions of designation, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may revoke its designation as a guarantee organization.
  (5) Where the Minister of Trade, and Industry or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to revoke designation of a defense contractor or guarantee organization pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (4), he or she shall hold a hearing. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) Matters necessary for the procedures, etc. of revocation of designation pursuant to paragraphs (1), (3), and (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER VII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 49 (Replacement, supplementation, expansion, or relocation of facilities) (1) Where there arises a dire need for national defense in a state of war, disaster or other emergency equivalent thereto, the Minister of Trade, and Industry may, at the request of the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, order the operator of a defense enterprise to take measures necessary for the replacement, supplementation, expansion, or relocation of the facilities directly provided by such enterprise to the manufacture of defense materials. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Trade, and Industry shall compensate for any loss arising from the replacement, supplementation, expansion, or relocation of the facilities due to an order pursuant to paragraph (1). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The person who has succeeded to the business which has the facilities with an order for replacement, supplementation, expansion, or relocation pursuant to paragraph (1), shall succeed to the rights and obligations under paragraphs (1) and (2) resulting from the order.

Article 50 (Duty of confidentiality) None of the following persons shall divulge or stealthily use any secrets he or she has learned in the course of performing his or her duties regarding a defense acquisition program: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2010; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. A person who held or is holding the post falling under Article 6(1)1 and 2;
  2. A person who has been commissioned as an ombudsman pursuant to Article 6(10);
  3. A person who is or was a representative, executive officer or employee at the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, a defense contractor, general enterprise, specialized research institute, or general research institute;
  4. A person who engaged or is engaging in the business of the manufacture and research of defense materials at the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, a defense contractor, general enterprise, specialized research institute, or general research institute;
  5. The counter-party to a defense acquisition contract, subcontractors, and former or current representatives or executive officers and employees of sub-subcontractors that enter into sub-subcontracts with subcontractors;

Article 50-2 (Approval for participation in national strategic arms projects) (1) When a company, the management right of which is substantially acquired by a foreign company or foreigner intends to participate in national strategic arms projects or other projects equivalent thereto, the company shall in advance obtain the approval from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, for the purpose of preventing science technology for national defense from being leaked overseas.
  (2) Standards, etc. for the kinds of projects subject to approval, procedure and time of approval, and substantial acquisition of the management right referred to in paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 31, 2010]

Article 50-3 (Approval for appointment of foreigners and person with multiple nationalities as executive officers of defense contractors) (1) In order to prevent defense technology (referring to defense technology under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Defense Technology Security Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) from being leaked overseas, a defense contractor that intends to appoint a foreigner or a person with multiple nationalities (referring to a person with multiple nationalities under Article 11-2(1) of the Nationality Act) as an executive officer or employ such person as an employee handling defense technology shall file an application with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration in advance and obtain approval.
  (2) Upon receipt of an application for approval under paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall review the impact on the protection of defense technology and notify the relevant defense contractor whether approval is granted within 60 days from the date of application. In such cases, the Administrator may attach conditions deemed necessary when granting approval.
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures, methods, criteria, etc. for approval under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 10, 2026]

Article 51 (Consultation on manufacture and sales contracts for defense materials) (1) Where a government agency or a person other than a government agency needs defense materials for the purposes of maintaining national security, guarding, research, testing, or inspection, etc., it may purchase them after concluding a manufacture or sales contract for defense materials with a defense contractor.
  (2) A government agency which intends to purchase defense materials under paragraph (1) shall consult with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration in advance, and the person other than the government agency shall obtain approval from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration upon recommendation by the head of the relevant central administrative agency; provided, an enterprise and defense contractor which have obtained a manufacturer's license regarding military firearms, swords, explosives, etc. pursuant to Article 53(1) need not receive the recommendation from the head of the relevant central administrative agency.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 1, 2009]

Article 51-2 (Fees) (1) Any of the following persons shall pay a fee:
  1. A person who intends to obtain certification of quality management pursuant to Article 29-2(2);
  2. A person who intends to renew certification of quality management pursuant to Article 29-2(3).
  (2) Matters necessary for persons subject to imposition of fees, the amount of a fee, payment methods, the payment period, etc. of fees under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 20, 2016]

Article 52 Deleted. <Mar. 31, 2020>

Article 53 (Special exceptions for manufacture of military firearms, knives, swords, explosives) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall permit and supervise the manufacturing, importation, exportation, transfer, acquisition, possession, use, storage, transportation, scrapping, etc. of military firearms, knives, swords, explosives, etc., and issue orders, or take measures, necessary therefor, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, notwithstanding the provisions of other statutes or regulations.
  (2) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraph (1), the Act on the Safety Management of Guns, Swords, and Explosives shall apply mutatis mutandis to military firearms, knives, swords, explosives, etc. <Amended on Jan. 6, 2015>

Article 54 (Orders for sale) (1) Where there arises a dire need for national defense in time of war, disaster or other emergencies equivalent thereto, or the operator of a defense enterprise or a person who has defense materials for sale brings about serious danger to national security by refusing to manufacture or sell defense materials, etc. without justifiable grounds, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may order the operator of the defense enterprise or the person who has defense materials for sale to transfer the defense materials to the Government by fixing the time and price of transfer, time and method of payment, and other necessary matters.
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration cannot issue an order for transfer pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1) because he or she cannot ascertain the owner of defense materials, he or she may order the person in possession of the defense materials concerned to deliver over them by fixing the time and price of delivery, time and method of payment, and other necessary matters.
  (3) The cost of manufacture, the profits of the enterprise, etc. shall be considered when determining the transfer price pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1).

Article 55 (Reserve of raw materials) (1) Defense contractors shall reserve raw materials for the manufacture of defense materials.
  (2) The administration of reserving of raw materials pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 56 (Suspension or cessation of business) (1) When a defense contractor intends to temporarily or permanently close his or her business, he or she shall obtain prior approval from the Minister of Trade, and Industry; provided, when a defense contractor intends to temporarily or permanently close his or her business because he or she is unable to conduct business normally due to insolvency, bankruptcy, or other unavoidable business circumstances, he or she shall file a report thereon with the Minister of Trade, and Industry. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 16, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) When the Minister of Trade, and Industry intends to grant approval under the main clause of paragraph (1), the Minister shall consult with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Added on Jan. 16, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 57 (Permission for exportation) (1) A person who intends to export or intermediate the trade of (including intermediating the trade between third party countries) defense materials and defense technology for business purposes shall report thereon to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  (2) A person who intends to export or intermediate the trade of defense materials and defense technology, shall obtain permission therefor from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, this shall not apply to cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, in which cases defense materials and defense technology are exported abroad to provide defense materials and defense technology for the Republic of Korea troops sent overseas. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Mar. 27, 2015; Dec. 20, 2016>
  (3) A person who intends to hold an export consulting meeting for major defense materials and defense technology shall obtain preliminary export approval from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, and each person, who intends to participate in international bidding, shall obtain approval for participation in international bidding from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  (4) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may place limitations on, or order an adjustment of, the exportation of defense materials and defense technology after consultation with the head of the relevant administrative agency, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  (5) A person who exports defense materials and defense technology without obtaining permission from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration pursuant to the proviso of paragraph (2) shall submit the current status of export transactions to the Administrator within seven days after the exportation thereof. <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016>
  [Title Amended on Mar. 27, 2015]

Article 57-2 (Registration of military supply sales agency business) (1) A person who intends to conduct military supply sales agency business shall register military supply sales agency business with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, none of the following persons shall obtain registration: <Amended on Jan. 7, 2025>
  1. A minor or a person under adult guardianship;
  2. A person for whom five years have not passed since his or her imprisonment without prison labor or heavier punishment as declared by a court was completely executed (including where the execution thereof is deemed completed) or exempted;
  3. A person who is under the suspension of the execution or sentence of imprisonment without prison labor or heavier punishment as declared by a court;
  4. A person for whom two years have not passed since the revocation of his or her registration because he or she fell under Article 57-3(1).
  (2) Where a person who has registered military supply sales agency business pursuant to paragraph (1) intends to alter any important matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, he or she shall register any alterations thereof.
  (3) Where a person obtains registration and registration of any alterations under paragraphs (1) and (2), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall issue a registration certificate to the applicant.
  (4) The effective period of registration under paragraph (1) shall be three years from the date of registration.
  (5) Matters necessary for procedures and methods for obtaining registration and registration of alterations, the issuance of a registration certificate, etc. under the provisions of paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 29, 2016]

Article 57-3 (Revocation of registration of military supply sales agency business) (1) Where a person who has registered military supply sales agency business falls under any of the following cases, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may revoke the registration of his or her military supply sales agency business; provided,, where the person falls under subparagraph 1, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall revoke the registration thereof: <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016>
  1. Where he or she obtains registration or registration of alterations by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Notwithstanding the fact that important matters under Article 57-2(2) have been altered among the matters registered, where he or she conducts military supply sales agency business without obtaining registration of alterations;
  3. Where he or she fails to comply with the details of a pledge of integrity he or she submitted pursuant to Article 6(1);
  4. Where he or she fails to report a commission, in violation of Article 57-4(1) or to report the alteration of a commission, in violation of Article 57-4(2), or makes a false report or a false report of the alteration of a commission.
  (2) Where a military supply sales agent participates in the course of performing a contract as a military supply sales agent even though the registration of his or her military supply sales agency business is revoked pursuant to paragraph (1), such military supply sales agent shall be deemed a military supply sales agent to the extent that the contract is terminated.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters necessary for the revocation of registration of military supply sales agency business shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 29, 2016]

Article 57-4 (Reporting of commission) (1) A person who conducts military supply sales agency business has entered into a contract concerning remuneration, such as a commission, (hereinafter referred to as "commission") with a foreign enterprise through the intermediation or agency with respect to a program larger than a scale prescribed by Presidential Decree, he or she shall report a commission to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (2) Where any alterations are made to matters reported, a person who has made a report pursuant to paragraph (1) shall report such altered matters to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (3) No current or former public official belonging to the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall use information about a commission he or she becomes aware of in the course of performing any of his or her duties, for the fraudulent purpose other than official purposes.
  (4) Matters necessary for methods and procedures for reporting a commission, the deadline for making a report, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 20, 2016]

Article 58 (Recovery of fraudulent gains) (1) Where a defense contractor, general enterprise, general enterprise which has no relationship with the defense industry, specialized research institute, or general research institute has made fraudulent gains by submitting data containing false or unlawful cost accounting to the Government, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall recover the fraudulent gains and additional dues equivalent to not more than double the fraudulent gains, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Dec. 20, 2016>
  (2) Criteria and methods for calculating additional dues referred to in paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree in consideration of the degree of an unfair practice and whether a defense contractor, etc. has made a report of its own accord. <Added on Dec. 20, 2016>
  [Title Amended on Dec. 20, 2016]

Article 59 (Restrictions on qualification for participation in bidding procedures) (1) Where a person specified in Article 6(1)4 falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may impose restrictions on the qualification for participation in bidding procedures for up to five years as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  1. Where a person promises or offers money, valuables, entertainment, etc. to relevant public officials (including members of the Committee referred to in Article 6(1)2) in charge of decision-making, bids, successful bids, and the conclusion and performance of contracts;
  2. Where a bidder or a counter-party to a contract agrees on a bid price, volume of orders, or the content of the contract in advance in consultation with other bidders or other counter-parties to the contract in the course of competitive bidding, or concluding or performing the contract, or where the person colludes with others for a successful bid by a specific person or selection of the subject of delivery of goods;
  3. Where a person has made an intercession or solicitation that hinders impartiality in performing duties;
  4. Where a person has requested any relevant public official to provide information related to a defense acquisition program or has received such information, or has arbitrarily provided or divulged to a third party information including research outcome that the person has learned in the course of performing a contract;
  5. Where a person has received money or valuables from a subcontractor, or has committed any illegal act or unfair practices, by taking advantage of his or her dominant position as a prime contractor when he or she enters into or performs a subcontract related to a defense acquisition program;
  6. Cases of leakage or infringement of defense technology or security incidents under the Defense Technology Security Act or the Military Secret Protection Act.
  (2) The Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall not conclude a negotiated contract with a person who is restricted from participating in bidding procedures pursuant to paragraph (1); provided, this shall not apply where unavoidable grounds exist, such as the absence of an appropriate manufacturer except for the person who is restricted from participating in bidding procedures pursuant to paragraph (1).
  (3) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration places restrictions on the qualification for participation in bidding procedures under paragraph (1)1 or 2, he or she shall immediately notify the head of another central government agency of such restrictions; provided, where the period of restrictions on the qualification for participation in bidding procedures exceeds two years, a notice shall be given as two years.
  (4) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the Minister of National Defense or the Minister of Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall not restrict the qualification for participation in bidding procedures in cases where five years have elapsed from the time the violation specified in each subparagraph of paragraph (1) ended; provided, the period for restrictions on the qualification for participation in bidding procedures imposed for any act of violation specified in paragraph (1)1 or 2 shall be seven years from the date the relevant violation ended.
  (5) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration restricts the qualification for participation in bidding procedures pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall disclose the details thereof, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Oct. 31, 2023]

Article 59-2 (Confirmation of persons subject to examination for fitness for employment by defense contractors) (1) Where a defense contractor falling under Article 17(1)1 or 12 of the Public Service Ethics Act intends to employ a person to whom the employment restriction period under Article 17(1) of that Act applies (hereinafter referred to as "person subject to examination for fitness for employment"), who is a retiree of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, among those obligated to obtain registration under that Act, it shall request the person subject to examination for fitness for employment to confirm whether he or she is subject to employment restrictions under Article 18 of that Act and confirm the result of examination on an application for approval of employment submitted by him or her. <Amended on Dec. 3, 2019>
  (2) No defense contractor specified in paragraph (1) shall employ a person whose employment is restricted pursuant to the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 17(1) of the Public Service Ethics Act or a person subject to examination for fitness for employment who fails to obtain confirmation that there are no close relations or to obtain approval of employment under the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of that paragraph. <Amended on Dec. 3, 2019>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 20, 2016]

Article 60 (Legal fiction of public officials) (1) A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs but who is not a public official shall be deemed a public official for purposes of applying penalty provisions of the Criminal Act and other statutes: <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. Members of the Committee, subcommittees, or working committees;
  2. A person who has been commissioned as an ombudsman pursuant to Article 6(10);
  3. Members of the committees prescribed by Presidential Decree, who perform preliminary examinations and advisory services on bids or successful bids related to defense acquisition contracts, or conclusion or performance of defense acquisition contracts.
  (2) The provisions of Chapter VII of the State Public Officials Act, regarding public service, and the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public Officials' Councils shall apply mutatis mutandis to the executives and employees of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, and they shall be deemed public officials for the purposes of applying the penalty provisions of the Criminal Act and other statutes. <Amended on Dec. 30, 2025>

Article 61 (Delegation and entrustment of authority) (1) Part of the authority of the Minister of National Defense under this Act may be delegated to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Part of the authority of the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration under this Act may be delegated to the President of the Agency for Defense Development and to the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>

CHAPTER VIII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 62 (Penalty provisions) (1) Deleted. <Feb. 4, 2020>
  (2) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with or without labor of up to 10 years or by a fine not exceeding 100 million won. <Amended on May 9, 2014; Dec. 20, 2016; Jan. 16, 2024>
  1. A person who produces, manufactures, or processes counterfeit parts, etc., or imports, sells, or uses counterfeit parts, etc. knowing that they are counterfeit parts, etc., in violation of Article 28-2(2);
  2. A person who obtains approval under Article 53 or the main clause of Article 57(2) by fraud or other improper means;
  3. A person who engages in the relevant activities without obtaining approval under Article 53 or the main clause of Article 57(2).
  (3) A person who divulges or pirates confidential information that he or she has learned while performing any of his or her duties in violation of Article 50, shall be punished by imprisonment, with or without labor, for not more than five years, or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won. <Amended on May 9, 2014>
  (4) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment, with or without labor, for not more than three years, or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won: <Amended on May 9, 2014; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. Deleted; < May 24, 2011>
  2. A person who uses a retaining fee or progress payment received pursuant to Article 46-2(1), for a purpose other than the original purpose;
  3. A person who performs an act specified in Article 48(1)12;
  4. A person who violates an order issued pursuant to Article 49(1), 53, or 54.
  (5) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor of up to one year, or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on Apr. 1, 2009; May 9, 2014; May 29, 2016; Dec. 20, 2016; Jan. 16, 2024; Jul. 22, 2025>
  1. A person who acquires a substantial controlling interest in management without obtaining approval provided for in the main clause of Article 35(3);
  1-2. A person who produces defense materials or modifies or develops defense materials and possesses or disposes of such defense materials without obtaining approval under Article 41(3);
  2. A person who uses State-owned property or goods for other purposes, in violation of Article 45(3);
  3. A person who uses defense materials for other purposes, which have been purchased by concluding a contract for manufacture and sales pursuant to Article 51(1);
  4. A person who suspends or closes his or her business without obtaining approval under the main clause of Article 56(1);
  5. A person who conducts military supply sales agency business without obtaining registration, in violation of Article 57-2(1), or registration of alterations, in violation of Article 57-2(2), obtains registration or registration of alterations by fraud or other improper means;
  6. A person who fails to make a report of a commission, in violation of Article 57-4(1), or to make a report of the alteration of a commission, in violation of Article 57-4(2), or makes a false report or false report of the alteration thereof;
  7. A person who uses information about a commission for fraudulent purposes, in violation of Article 57-4(3).
  (6) Any of the following persons shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won: <Amended on Mar. 27, 2015>
  1. A person who fails to reserve raw materials for the manufacture of defense materials provided for in Article 55 without good cause;
  2. A person who engages in export business of defense materials without reporting provided for in Article 57(1), or who reports export business of defense materials by fraud or other improper means.

Article 63 (Joint penalty provisions) Where the representative of a corporation, or an agent, servant, or other employee of a corporation or individual has committed an offense provided for in Article 62 with regard to the affairs of the corporation or individual, such corporation or individual shall be fined pursuant to the provisions of Article 62, in addition to the punishment of the offender; provided, the same shall not apply where such corporation or individual has not been negligent in giving due attention and supervision concerning the relevant duties to prevent such offense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 1, 2009]

Article 64 (Administrative fines) (1) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding five million won: <Amended on Jan. 16, 2024>
  1. A person who suspends or closes his or her business without filing a report under the proviso of Article 56(1);
  2. A person who fails to verify the results of an examination, in violation of Article 59-2(1).
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall impose and collect administrative fines under paragraph (1), as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 20, 2016]

